WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar, and you're watching Mind Your Decisions.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:08.130
Oxford ranks as one of the top universities in the world.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:13.290
A version of this problem was used as an Oxford interview question.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.590
A ladder is placed vertically on a wall.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:24.330
What shape does the midpoint of the ladder trace as the ladder slides against the wall to the floor?

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:31.289
What if the ladder instead fell backwards to the ground by rotating from its base?

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:35.399
What shape would the midpoint of the ladder trace then?

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:38.280
Can you figure it out?

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:42.599
Give this problem a try, and when you're ready keep watching the video for a solution.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.000
So, how can we solve this problem?

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:07.860
Well, it sometimes helps in problems like these to sketch out a few cases and get some intuition of what the answer might be.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:13.680
So let's place this ladder vertically on the wall and consider the path of its midpoint.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:21.270
We can imagine the ladder sliding against the wall and we'll keep track of where the midpoint of this ladder goes.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:25.589
Eventually, we'll get some idea of what this path is.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:32.099
Now focusing just on the midpoints we might guess that this shape is a quarter circle

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:39.539
But Oxford interviews - just like my videos - are about more than getting just the correct answer.

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:44.579
It's about demonstrating your ability to think and to prove the answer.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:48.360
So can we prove that the path is a quarter circle?

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:55.319
Well, in order to do that, let's go back to our sketch and consider one position of the ladder.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.770
Let's write L for the length of the ladder.

00:02:00.399 --> 00:02:06.479
Above and below the midpoint will be L/2 because we have half the ladders length in each direction.

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:09.719
Now consider these two right triangles.

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:18.839
Because they have the same angles and their hypotenuse has the same length these two right triangles are congruent to each other.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:24.569
So they will have X as their horizontal length and Y as the vertical length

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:29.130
Now the midpoint will be (x, y) in a coordinate system.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.220
Now looking at either triangle,

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:40.320
we can use the Pythagorean Theorem
to get x² + y² = (L/2)².

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:48.419
This equation is very well-known. In this case the midpoint traces out a quarter circle in the positive quadrant

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:55.349
with the radius that's half the ladder's length. And this is the proof of why the path is a quarter circle.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.369
But there's nothing quite like seeing it for yourself

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:06.779
So here's an animation that I made on Desmos.com, and you can see that the midpoint of the ladder

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:12.509
exactly traces out the path a quarter-circle as it slides against the wall.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.729
So now let's consider the second problem,

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.399
which is the ladder falling away from the wall.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:27.240
Like before let's sketch each of these cases and get an idea for the path of the midpoint.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:34.080
Now looking at this path we might once again guess that it's a quarter circle.

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:36.810
But can we prove it?

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:39.419
Well, in this case it's a little bit easier:

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:46.799
The midpoint will always be a distance of L/2 from the corner of the wall in the ground.

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:51.719
So you can imagine that's the same distance and therefore it's a quarter circle.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:56.910
But we can also prove it like in the first case where we use the Pythagorean Theorem

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:03.149
and we get exactly the same equations as before to show that this midpoint also traces out

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:05.280
the path of a quarter circle.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:15.779
So once again, seeing is also satisfying so here's an animation that's made with Desmos.com to show

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:21.719
that the midpoint traces out the path of a quarter circle as the ladder falls away from the wall.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.450
And we now have a rather curious result:

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:28.380
Both cases show the same path of the midpoint!

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:33.359
So here's another animation that puts both ladders in

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:38.009
the same graph and you'll see the midpoint of each ladder

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:40.530
is along this quarter circle.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:48.720
So amazingly, both cases result in the same path of a quarter circle.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:57.280
Now obviously in real life the sliding ladder will be slower due to its friction against the wall in the ground.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:03.200
This animation is just to show why the paths of the midpoints are the same shape.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:11.240
A geometric way that you can see this is that the two ladders represent the diagonals of a rectangle,

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:15.660
and they will intersect at this midpoint of the two ladders.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:20.370
You can also see this as the pivot point of a scissor lift.

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:22.950
So it's quite amazing

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:29.309
that in either case the midpoint of the ladder is tracing out the same quarter circle

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.400
Did you figure it out?

